{"name":"Loblolly","hex":"#b3bbb7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"loblolly","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Gruel.","A thick stew; lobscouse or similar."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To behave in a loutish manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/loblolly"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pinus taeda","blocks":[{"text":"Pinus taeda, commonly known as loblolly pine, is a species of conifer in the family Pinaceae, a pine native to the Southeastern United States, from East Texas to Florida, up to southern New Jersey. The wood industry classifies the species as a southern yellow pine. U.S. Forest Service surveys found that loblolly pine is the second-most common species of tree in the United States, after red maple. For its timber, the pine species is regarded as the most commercially important tree in the Southeastern U.S. The common name loblolly is given because the pine species is found mostly in lowlands and swampy areas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Loblolly_Pines_South_Mississippi.JPG/330px-Loblolly_Pines_South_Mississippi.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinus_taeda","wikidata":"Q368248"},{"title":"Gordonia lasianthus","blocks":[{"text":"Gordonia lasianthus is a small to medium-sized evergreen tree or shrub found in acidic, swampy soils of pinelands and bays on the Atlantic and Gulf Coastal Plains of the southeastern United States. It is a member of the tea or family Theaceae. It is slow growing with soft, light-colored, fine-grained wood of little commercial value, although loblolly-bay could be managed as a source of pulpwood. When older specimens are cut, the wood exudes a strong scent. It is similar to a mixture of fresh oranges and pine sap. The bark of the adult tree varies from medium grey to a red brown coloration. Dead adult specimens of loblolly bay exhibit a lustrous shine when exposed to sunlight for several years. The white showy flowers and shiny foliage make it a desirable ornamental, but it is not easy to cultivate. Deer browse stump sprouts heavily."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/32/Gordonia_lasianthus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gordonia_lasianthus","wikidata":"Q5586051"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Pinus_taeda_cones.jpg/250px-Pinus_taeda_cones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Pinus_taeda_cones.jpg/500px-Pinus_taeda_cones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Pinus_taeda_cones.jpg/250px-Pinus_taeda_cones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APinus_taeda_cones.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Tree_Types_and_Barks_004.jpg/250px-Tree_Types_and_Barks_004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Tree_Types_and_Barks_004.jpg/330px-Tree_Types_and_Barks_004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Tree_Types_and_Barks_004.jpg/250px-Tree_Types_and_Barks_004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATree_Types_and_Barks_004.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/P._taeda_old_growth.jpg/250px-P._taeda_old_growth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/